Start the external filter settled, not charging.

A Commodore 64 couples its audio out through a DC blocker, which reSIDfp
models as a 1.6 Hz high-pass in ExternalFilter. Upstream resets both of its
networks to zero, so every tune begins with the filter uncharged and the
chip's whole DC pedestal reaches the output until the high-pass drains it,
which takes the better part of a second.

That used to be a harmless thump. libsidplayfp 2.6.0 amplified the signal
path ("residfp: amplify the output by a model dependent scale factor", and
2.5.0 "tweaked the 8580 filter parameters for increased volume"), and there
is no longer headroom for the pedestal on top: measured on an 8580 tune, the
first second peaked at full scale with about 480 samples of 44,100 clipped
and a mean sample value of 0.168, settling afterwards to a peak of 0.236.
The same tune through libsidplayfp 2.4.2 peaks at 0.20 from the first sample
and never clips.

With a constant input both networks converge on the input voltage, so the
settled state is simply Vlp = Vhp = Vi. This seeds them from the first sample
after a reset rather than guessing a constant per chip model and volume
setting: whatever DC the chip is producing, the filter starts there, and one
sample of silence is returned in place of the step.

The alternatives were worse. An output gain would have to be about 12 dB to
clear a transient four times the steady level, making the whole collection
quiet to fix the first second of it; a second DC blocker in the player would
stack another high-pass on the one already modelled here, altering the bass
of everything, and would shorten the step rather than remove it.

Reset covers subsong changes and tune switches too: the worklet reloads the
tune for both, and SID::reset() calls ExternalFilter::reset().

Applies to libsidplayfp 2.6.0. Verified by scripts/verify-engine.mjs, which
renders the first second of a tune that asks for an 8580 and fails if any
sample reaches full scale.

--- a/src/builders/residfp-builder/residfp/ExternalFilter.h
+++ b/src/builders/residfp-builder/residfp/ExternalFilter.h
@@ -70,6 +70,13 @@
     /// Highpass filter voltage
     int Vhp;
 
+    /**
+     * False until the first sample after a reset has been seen, so that both
+     * networks can start charged to whatever DC the chip is actually putting
+     * out rather than ramping up to it from nothing.
+     */
+    bool primed;
+
     int w0lp_1_s7;
 
     int w0hp_1_s17;
@@ -111,6 +118,25 @@
 int ExternalFilter::clock(int input)
 {
     const int Vi = (input<<11) - (1 << (11+15));
+
+    if (!primed)
+    {
+        /*
+         * Start settled instead of charging up over the first second.
+         *
+         * With a constant input both networks converge on Vi, so seeding them
+         * from the first sample puts the filter where it would have ended up
+         * anyway. Starting from zero instead passes the chip's whole DC
+         * pedestal through until the 1.6 Hz high-pass drains it, and since
+         * libsidplayfp 2.6.0 amplified the signal path there is no longer the
+         * headroom for that: the first second of an 8580 tune clipped.
+         */
+        primed = true;
+        Vlp = Vi;
+        Vhp = Vi;
+        return 0;
+    }
+
     const int dVlp = (w0lp_1_s7 * (Vi - Vlp) >> 7);
     const int dVhp = (w0hp_1_s17 * (Vlp - Vhp) >> 17);
     Vlp += dVlp;
--- a/src/builders/residfp-builder/residfp/ExternalFilter.cpp
+++ b/src/builders/residfp-builder/residfp/ExternalFilter.cpp
@@ -60,9 +60,10 @@
 
 void ExternalFilter::reset()
 {
-    // State of filter.
+    // State of filter. Seeded from the first sample instead, in clock().
     Vlp = 0; //1 << (15 + 11);
     Vhp = 0;
+    primed = false;
 }
 
 } // namespace reSIDfp
